# Samples packaged for the browser, one per line:
#
#   board:app_id:zephyr_sample_path[:conf,conf,...[:snippet,snippet,...]]
#
# - board            Zephyr board target (west build -b ...)
# - app_id           artifact basename -> public/qemu/zephyr/<board>/<app_id>.elf
#                    Must match a sample id listed for the board in src/boards.ts.
# - zephyr_sample_path  relative to the zephyr/ tree, or one of this repo's own
#                    apps when it starts with "zephyr-module/"
# - conf             optional Kconfig fragments, relative to zephyr-module/,
#                    comma-separated
# - snippet          optional snippets (west build -S ...), comma-separated and
#                    applied in the order written, so a bus comes before the
#                    parts on it. Leave the conf field empty (a bare "::") to
#                    use snippets without fragments.
#
# THE SNIPPET LIST IS THE SAMPLE'S BILL OF MATERIALS. The browser_bridge shield
# declares every browser-fed device disabled and says only where it sits on the
# machine; each line below is what solders the parts on. Keep it to what the
# sample can actually exercise — the device dock is grounded in the running
# build's flattened devicetree (docs/peripherals.md), so an extra node is a
# peripheral card the user has to look past, not a free unused driver.
#
# Bridges, from zephyr-module/snippets/:
#   gnss ......... NMEA receiver on a spare UART
#   host-audio ... I2S out into Web Audio          (pairs with conf/host-audio.conf)
#   host-mic ..... DMIC in from getUserMedia       (pairs with conf/host-mic.conf)
#   host-gpio .... qemu,host-gpio + LED/button     (Cortex-M3; pairs with conf/host-gpio.conf)
#   net .......... virtio-net, or the Stellaris MAC on the M3 (pairs with conf/net.conf)
#   ramfb ........ the 600x400 framebuffer         (virt machines)
#   virtio-input . the tablet behind the display   (virt machines; pairs with conf/touch.conf)
#   virtio-gpio .. VIRTIO GPIO + its LED/button    (virt machines)
#   esp32-i2c .... the ESP32-C3's own I2C controller, with the same parts bin
#                  under the same node labels, so every `<part>-only` below
#                  works on that board too (the chips are the same page-side
#                  models; only the bus underneath differs)
#   virtio-i2c ... VIRTIO I2C adapter; declares every chip the page models, all
#                  disabled, and a `<part>-only` snippet solders one on:
#                  adxl345-only, eeprom-only, lsm6dso-only, lps22hh-only,
#                  ina219-only, isl29035-only, pcf8523-only, jhd1313-only,
#                  ht16k33-only, lp5562-only, lp5012-only, pca9685-only,
#                  mcp4725-only, max17048-only — or i2c-shell for the whole set
#                  the shell's commands can drive
#   esp32-can .... the ESP32-C3's own TWAI controller on the browser's CAN bus
#                  (no bridge chip: TWAI is the SoC's real CAN peripheral)
#   esp32-spi .... the ESP32-C3's own GP-SPI2 with the same JEDEC NOR on CS0
#   virtio-spi ... VIRTIO SPI + a JEDEC NOR on CS0; self-contained alternatives
#                  on the same bus are pt6314-only, sct2024-only, ws2812-only,
#                  tmc50xx-only, mcp2515-only, bt-settings-spi
#   virtio-blk ... a stock QEMU block device (no page-side model)
#   virtio-gpu ... the display behind virtio-gpu instead of ramfb
#   oled ......... SSD1306 on the I2C bus, as `zephyr,display` instead of ramfb
#   auxdisplays .. every auxdisplay the shell can drive (I2C LCD, SPI VFD, 7-seg)
#   accel-display  shrink ramfb to 480x320 for the accelerometer chart
#   bt-hci-uart .. H:4 controller on a spare UART
#   cpu-power-states  a ladder of CPU suspend states for CONFIG_PM to walk
#   pm-demo-devices   three devices that implement PM, so the system-managed
#                  device walk has something to suspend
#   browser-tracing, browser-tracing-net — added automatically, see below
#
# Cortex-A53 focus (docs/focus.md): build-zephyr-image.sh expands every A53
# entry into a twin with id <app>_trace that adds snippets browser-tracing
# (and browser-tracing-net when conf/net.conf is present), except samples that
# already embed CTF in their own prj.conf (tracing, tracing_pipeline). Do not
# list *_trace ids here — the UI mirrors the same expansion in src/boards.ts.
#
# Rebuild everything with:
#
#   tools/build-zephyr-image.sh
#
# Cortex-M3 runs on qemu-wasm's TCI interpreter. Most samples run — including
# single-threaded sleepers like blinky and basic_button, though their timing is
# not wall-clock accurate — but a few multi-threaded ones (philosophers,
# synchronization) stall. Cortex-A53 uses the wasm JIT and has no such limit.
# qemu_riscv32 uses TCI on RISC-V virt with the same virtio bridges as A53
# (tools/qemu-riscv-patches/); build it with
# `tools/build-qemu-wasm.sh riscv32-softmmu`.
#
# Networking samples talk to the browser page, which implements the whole
# LAN behind the QEMU "browser" netdev: it answers as gateway/DHCP/SNTP
# (192.0.2.2), DNS (192.0.2.3), and every remote host at once. conf/net.conf
# keeps the guests IPv4-only and on the Ethernet (not SLIP) QEMU path.

qemu_cortex_m3:gnss:samples/drivers/gnss::gnss
qemu_cortex_m3:shell:samples/subsys/shell/shell_module:conf/host-gpio.conf,conf/host-audio.conf:host-gpio,host-audio
qemu_cortex_m3:hsm:samples/subsys/smf/hsm_psicc2
qemu_cortex_m3:dhcp:samples/net/dhcpv4_client:conf/net.conf:net
qemu_cortex_m3:http_server:samples/net/sockets/dumb_http_server:conf/net.conf:net
qemu_cortex_m3:http_get:samples/net/sockets/http_get:conf/net.conf:net
qemu_cortex_m3:hello_world:samples/hello_world
qemu_cortex_m3:blinky:samples/basic/blinky::host-gpio
qemu_cortex_m3:basic_button:samples/basic/button::host-gpio
qemu_cortex_m3:buzzer:samples/drivers/buzzer/tone:conf/buzzer.conf:host-gpio,gpio-buzzer
qemu_cortex_m3:stepper:samples/drivers/stepper/generic:conf/stepper.conf:host-gpio,gpio-step-dir

